## MPI on the Head Node

The {{ provisioner_name }} services run in podman networks, so the head node
carries their bridge interfaces (`podman0`, `podman1`, ...) next to its real
ones. libfabric's TCP-based providers enumerate every interface that is up,
and MPICH can pick one of the bridges as its default NIC. An MPI program
started on the head node then fails with an error such as:

```
OFI call tsenddata failed (default nic=podman3: No such file or directory)
```

Jobs run through the resource manager are not affected, because the compute
nodes carry no podman interfaces. Running MPI programs on the head node
itself is unusual; the OpenHPC test suite does it, and pins those tests to
libfabric's *tcp* provider on the loopback interface. Pinning the interface
alone does not help, because MPICH can select another provider, for example
*sockets*, which ignores `FI_TCP_IFACE`. For an interactive MPI run on the
head node, set both for that session:

```bash
export FI_PROVIDER=tcp
export FI_TCP_IFACE=${sms_eth_internal}
```

::: {.tip}
**Note:** job launchers forward the environment, so these variables set in
a login shell also reach the jobs submitted from it. On the compute nodes
they would force the tcp provider onto the named interface, which disables
a faster fabric. Do not set them permanently, for example in
`/etc/profile.d`.
:::
